INT. CONSERVATORY, FINCH-HATTON HOUSE - EARLY MORNING

Glass walls beaded with dew. Orchids hang from iron hooks. Rain taps the roof like fingernails on a table. A white sheet covers something long on the tiled floor.

INSPECTOR OSWIN PELLEGRINI, fifties, rumpled tweed, a pipe he does not light, stands over the sheet. Around him wait the household: LADY CORDELIA FINCH-HATTON, seventy, upright in a wheelchair; AMOS TREVELYAN, the butler, pale as candle wax; MRS. BERNADETTE QUILL, the cook, wringing a dishcloth; and JASPER, the nephew, twenty-two, slouched against a palm.

PELLEGRINI
Nobody leaves.

JASPER
We were having breakfast.

PELLEGRINI
Then you may have it later.

He crouches. He lifts the corner of the sheet and lets it fall. Lady Cordelia grips the arms of her chair. The cook turns her face to the glass.

PELLEGRINI (CONT'D)
(to himself)
No sign of a struggle.

AMOS
Inspector, if I might say.

PELLEGRINI
You might.

AMOS
The doors were locked at eleven.

MRS. QUILL
They were. I locked the kitchen.

JASPER
I locked nothing, I never lock anything.

CORDELIA
Be quiet, Jasper.

PELLEGRINI
Lady Finch-Hatton, who was the last to see him?

CORDELIA
I was. At half past ten.

PELLEGRINI
And?

CORDELIA
He was watering the orchids.

Pellegrini rises, slowly, and goes to the nearest hanging basket. He touches the soil with two fingers.

PELLEGRINI
(a murmur)
Dry.

Jasper and Amos exchange a look. Mrs. Quill drops the dishcloth. Cordelia's wheels creak as she rolls backward half a foot, and then stops.

PELLEGRINI (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Whoever killed him watered the plants after.

Silence. Rain. Then, softly, Lady Cordelia begins to laugh.
